  "description": "Compute the outer product of two vectors yielding a rank-one matrix of pairwise element multiplications.\n\nEvery matrix can be decomposed into a sum of rank-one outer product matrices via Singular Value Decomposition.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\nM_{ij} = u_i v_j, \\quad M = u v^T\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Verify vector dimensionality m and n\n2. Compute pairwise multiplications across all index combinations\n3. Return m x n rank-1 outer product matrix\n\n### Returns\nNested list representing outer product matrix.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Outer Product\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nLinear algebra is the foundational mathematical language of machine learning. Data samples, image pixels, and word embeddings are all represented as multidimensional vectors and matrices. **Outer Product** transforms coordinate systems, extracts principal axes of variation, or decomposes complex transformations into simpler geometric building blocks.\n\nEvery matrix can be decomposed into a sum of rank-one outer product matrices via Singular Value Decomposition. In this problem, the goal is to: compute the outer product of two vectors yielding a rank-one matrix of pairwise element multiplications.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\nM_{ij} = u_i v_j, \\quad M = u v^T\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Verify vector dimensionality m and n\n2. **Phase 2**: Compute pairwise multiplications across all index combinations\n3. **Phase 3**: Return m x n rank-1 outer product matrix\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Feature Compression & Projections**: Operations like low-rank approximations and orthogonal projections compress high-dimensional feature spaces while preserving maximum variance.\n* **Numerical Stability**: When inverting matrices or solving linear systems, ill-conditioned matrices (matrices with very small determinants or high condition numbers) require regularization to prevent numerical instability.\n* **Vectorized Execution**: Expressing computations as matrix multiplications allows GPUs and CPUs to leverage hardware tensor cores for maximum throughput.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: u = [1.0, 2.0, 3.0], v = [4.0, 5.0]\n\nOutput: [[4.0, 5.0], [8.0, 10.0], [12.0, 15.0]]\n\nExplanation: Evaluated la_outer_product under standard test conditions."
  ],
